WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.840 align:start position:0%
 
you  this morning people from around the  country are expected to gather in  Hampton Virginia to commemorate a  pivotal moment in American history this  month marks 400 years since the first  documented enslaved Africans arrived in  English North America the weekend events  kick off at the Tucker family cemetery  believed to be the burial place of some  of the descendants of the first enslaved  Africans to come here CBS this morning  national correspondent  Jericka Duncan visited that cemetery and  the Tucker family  Jericka how unique is their story very  unique Anthony it is rare for African  American families in this country to be  able to trace their lineage back to 1619  well the Tucker family believes it has  its family cemetery is ground zero for  where life ended but as we learned it is  also where the story begins  tucked beneath the trees in a quiet  neighborhood in Hampton Virginia is a  piece of American history that is rarely  seen or told like this so we had a  ground-penetrating radar  done and it went down six feet and what  did you find we found over a hundred and  four unmarked graves those unmarked  graves according to the Tucker family  are likely the remains of their  relatives who were among the first  enslaved Africans to arrive in English  North America in 1619 colonists John  Rolfe famously recorded the arrival of  twenty and odd Negroes who were captured  from Angola Africa and brought to the  shores of the Old Point Comfort Virginia  most of it is oral history that takes us  back that far  carolita Jones cope says that history  connects their family to Captain William  Tucker whose plantation was a  quarter-mile from where the Tucker  family cemetery lies today  Captain William Tucker was the commander  of Point Comfort and he kept two of  those servants Anthony and Isabella in  1624 it lists that Antony and Isabella  had a child William and that he was born  and baptized that was the start of our  family  legacy here in Virginia when you look at  this history that your family has been  able to preserve what is the  significance of knowing where you came  from and having the pictures having the  genealogy well one thing some  nice-looking family and and to look at  the way that they dress they walk with  the heads up you know with pride dignity  there were smart people they were strong  people they were hard-working people and  that just gives me so much pride knowing  that that's stock that I come from  uncovering their past has meant a lot to  this family why did the connections  matter what does it give you a sense of  knowing where you came from to now know  that my African ancestry started so long  ago and they survived like they  struggled and I can't imagine the  conditions that they lived in when they  were here but to know that we are still  here and surviving is just makes me so  proud because now we are trying to tell  our children this story and we want each  generation to keep this story going so  this is what we're doing with these  pictures are because this is our family  we face many challenges as people of  color African Americans so it helps  strengthens strengthen the next  generation moving forward to know  whether our ancestors have gone through  and did this family says tracing their  roots is not just a history lesson for  themselves but a cultural lesson that  will resonate worldwide when you look at  some of the debates that are happening  in this country today around race  relations around immigrants how does  this play into that bigger narrative I'm  sorry it's still a problem  that it's the the condition that we're  living under now and then we're still  trying to justify our existence here  because we we built this country we  helped to make it the Tucker family will  be featured in a new exhibit honoring  the first Africans landing at Point  Comfort the site was later renamed Fort  Monroe so there are over a hundred  crosses here at the cemetery yes which  represents unmarked graves that we were  able to identify doing the  ground-penetrating radar  so you had someone come here to find  potential bodies that you didn't know  we're here exactly when I started off I  I was just a young man who was looking  at this space as a very special space  and I just didn't think the nation would  pay attention Terry Brown is  Superintendent of Fort Monroe why do you  think it's finally getting attention  well I think slavery and race is a very  difficult subject for this nation but I  want to remind the entire nation that a  great country remembers its history and  it embraces all the complexities of it  what do you think that does for the  young people today when they know their  history and they're proud of it there's  nothing like knowing where you came from  there's nothing like it and for many  African Americans is very hard to go  beyond you know the civil rights  movement it's a very sad story in a lot  of ways but I'm here to tell everyone  that those Africans that unloaded here  in 1619 they were scientists they were  lawyers doctors they were not just  simply slaves they were human beings for  the Tucker family connecting back to  where they came from means embracing  where they are today we are not going  anyplace  we are here this this is where we belong  because our people helped to make  America what it is regardless of whether  they try to push us out on tell you to  go back home or whatever it is we are  home we are home  we are here efforts to commemorate 400  years of african-american history have  been embraced by both the Obama and  Trump administrations President Obama  named Fort Monroe a national monument in  2011 recognizing this landmarks role in  American history and last year President  Trump signed a law establishing a 15  member Commission to plan develop and  carry out programs highlighting the  contributions of African Americans in  this country become interested when you  were there were there other people  visiting or only the Tucker family well  this is an area for those who are  visiting for that reason they know to go  to the cemetery it's a 2.2 acres and it  is just so rare to have a family that  has held on to this history to be able  to go and find out that there were more  bodies than they expected and shout out  to Tony Furlow and Dexter Lyon who were  the sound and photographers they did an  outstanding job at that sign there that  said first Africans in Virginia looked a  little lonely and yeah it could use some  improving though people don't know about  the significance it seems like I don't  think so you know I'm actually planning  a family reunion this year in Hampton  Virginia is a place that I'm looking at  because there is so much rich history  there and you wouldn't know that I don't  think unless you you know are educating  yourself and a lot of people don't think  about the 1619 as a start yes I think  commemorating this year is very  important so I love what Terry Brown at  Fort Monroe said that a great country  rim remembers its history and it  embraces the complexities of it that's  right it is completely we have to learn  from the bad stuff but also celebrate  what's good this is such an incredible  story for this family to be able to  trace its roots so far back before most  people got to this country tell us a  little bit about the story of how those  Africans came to Virginia on a ship that  was actually bound for another color I  mean it was pirated and then it sort of  ends up here at Point Comfort which is  now Point Monroe and there's so many  rich stories there but again this family  was so gracious to share their pictures  their documents and hopefully others  will be encouraged to look up their  history and maybe give Hampton Virginia  some love  [Laughter]  you should do your family reunion there  I think the McQuaid's awkward side you  guys would get it alright look out for  that speaker thank you very much

